  abstract = {The Connes Embedding Problem (CEP) is a problem in the theory of tracial von Neumann algebras and asks whether or not every tracial von Neumann algebra embeds into an ultrapower of the hyperfinite II\$\_1\$ factor. The CEP has had interactions with a wide variety of areas of mathematics, including C*-algebra theory, geometric group theory, free probability, and noncommutative real algebraic geometry (to name a few). After remaining open for over 40 years, a negative solution was recently obtained as a corollary of a landmark result in quantum complexity theory known as \${\textbackslash}operatorname\{MIP\}{\textasciicircum}*={\textbackslash}operatorname\{RE\}\$. In these notes, we introduce all of the background material necessary to understand the proof of the negative solution of the CEP from \${\textbackslash}operatorname\{MIP\}{\textasciicircum}*={\textbackslash}operatorname\{RE\}\$. In fact, we outline two such proofs, one following the "traditional" route that goes via Kirchberg's QWEP problem in C*-algebra theory and Tsirelson's problem in quantum information theory and a second that uses basic ideas from logic.},
